#import "/_lib/0.1.1.typ": *
#show: template

#taxon[Lemma]
#title[Class Formula]

Let $X : B G -> "1-Type"$ be a finite group $G$, with base point $"sh"_G : B G$
acting on the 1-type $X("sh"_G)$. Then
$
  |sum_(s: B G) X(s)|  = (|X("sh"_G)|)/(|G|).
$


#proof[ To save keystrokes we use the abbreviation $T :-= sum_(z:B G) X(z) $.

  To compute the cardinality of $T$, we begin by showing that its connected
  components are the orbits of $G$'s action on the set truncation of $"sh"_G$.

  The action on $X("sh"_G)$ induces an action on $pi_0(X("sh"_G))$ as follows.
  Using the universal property of the set truncation it is enough to define our
  action for elements in $pi_0(X("sh"_G))$ of the form $|x|_0$ for some $x :
  X("sh"_G)$.
  $
    g * |x|_0 :-= |"tr"_X (l_g, x)|_0.
  $

  We now define an equivalence reation on $pi_0(X("sh"_G))$ that identifies two
  elements whenever they lie in the same orbit.
  $
    a ~ b :-= || sum_(g : G) g*a=b ||_(-1)
  $

  The quotient $pi_0(X("sh"_G)) slash G$ has the following univeral property:
  TODO

// #figure(diagram($
//   T edge(f, ->) edge("d", |\_|_0, ->) & S \
//   pi_0(T) edge("ur", tilde(f), "-->")
// $))
//
//  TODO: Universal property of the set quotient
// #figure(diagram($
//   pi_0(X(**)) edge(g, ->) edge("d", q, ->) & S' \
//   pi_0(X(**)) slash G edge("ur", tilde(g), "-->")
// $))

  TODO: Conclude with UP of set quotient and UP of set truncation that
  $
    pi_0(T) ~= pi_0(X("sh"_G)) slash G
  $

  We now move on to classify the automorphisms of the connected components.
  Consider a point in $T$ of the form $("sh"_G, x)$, then 
  $
    (("sh"_G,x) = ("sh"_G,x)) &~= sum_(p : "sh"_G = "sh"_G) "tr"_X (p,x)=x\
    & ~= sum_(g : U G) g*x = x
  $
]

